WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
So you've got left atrium and left

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.000
ventricle, and the ventricles and

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
the atria are separated by the AV valves,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.000
and they're held down by these, these are the

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.000
chordae tendineae, those strands right there.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.000
And then here is one of the flaps of the

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
bicuspid, and here is the other flap of

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
the bicuspid. That's how it gets its name.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Whereas if you look on the other side,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.000
this is the right atrium, and then this

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.000
is the right AV valve, and it has three

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
flaps to it, so it's often called the

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.000
tricuspid, but it's also all right to say

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
right AV valve, left AV valve.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Then here's the chordae tendineae again. The

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
chordae tendineae are held tightly down to the

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.000
bottom of the ventricles by these kind

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
of carrot-shaped muscles called

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.000
papillary

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
muscles. So papillary muscles, chordae tendineae,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
AV

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.000
valve. Now the next thing to find are the

00:00:59.000 --> 00:00:59.053
semilunar valves. So this is the
